    "result_text": "Full build is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0).\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented `namesakeCount` on the `POST /api/owners` create pipeline \u2014 the number of existing owners (before this create) sharing the same firstName **and** lastName, case-insensitively.\n\nChanges:\n\n1. **New function step** `AssignNamesakeCount.java` \u2014 counts existing owners via `OwnerRepository.findAll()` whose firstName and lastName both match the new owner's (normalized: trim, collapse whitespace, lower-case), then `owner.setNamesakeCount(count)`. It runs before `SaveOwner`, so the owner being created is never counted against itself.\n\n2. **Wired into** `owners.POST.yml` \u2014 inserted the `assignNamesake` step (under `transaction` governance) between `assignCode` and `save`.\n\n3. **`Owner` model** \u2014 added the `namesakeCount` field (`@Column(name = \"namesake_count\")`) with getter/setter, matching the existing `customerCode`/`householdId` pattern.\n\n4. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added a read-only `namesakeCount` integer property to the `Owner` schema; the generated `OwnerDto` is mapped from the entity automatically by MapStruct (by name), so `GET /api/owners/{id}` returns it.\n\n5. **DB schemas** \u2014 added `namesake_count INTEGER` to the `owners` table in all four dialects (hsqldb, h2, mysql, postgres), and appended the extra `NULL` to the positional seed inserts in `db/hsqldb/data.sql` (the only dialect using column-less `INSERT ... VALUES`; the others use explicit column lists and needed no change).\n\nThe provided `AcceptanceTest.coreCountsNamesakes` passes: two owners sharing `firstName`+`lastName` (distinct postcodes to avoid the household-duplicate 409) yield `namesakeCount = 1` on the second.",
